#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186543869 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6c58d987-961a-585e-a491-00bbed01b89a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543862}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186543862} :answer-field/id #uuid "1f76cdf7-8c2e-5196-9f1e-b93f95d0b0e2" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543847} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "29710f27-4a0c-540d-9afb-36c328129c2e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543848}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186543848} :answer-field/id #uuid "b5baf09d-53ca-5d62-bb06-c003ce6dd6fc" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543884}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "81fb6273-5dee-5beb-bfc9-3f4b77ae324c" :question/problem "For the ellipse $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{{x}^{2}}{\\frac{5}{4}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{\\frac{27}{4}}=1,\n$$ find the lengths of its major and minor axes.\n\n$\\,$ The length of the major axis is \n{{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ The length of the minor axis is \n{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "Remember that the equation of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn the given equation, we have ${a}^{2}=\\frac{5}{4}$ and ${b}^{2}=\\frac{27}{4}.$\n\n- Since ${a}^{2}=\\frac{5}{4},$ the length of the horizontal radius must be $$\n\\displaystyle a=\\sqrt{\\frac{5}{4}}=\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{2}.\n$$\n\n- Since ${b}^{2}=\\frac{27}{4},$ the length of the vertical radius must be $$\n\\displaystyle b=\\sqrt{\\frac{27}{4}}=\\frac{3\\sqrt{3}}{2}.\n$$\n\nThus, the semi-major axis has length $b=\\frac{3\\sqrt{3}}{2},$ and the semi-minor axis has length $a=\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{2}.$\n\nTherefore, the major axis has length $2b=2\\cdot \\frac{3\\sqrt{3}}{2}=3\\sqrt{3},$ and the minor axis has length $2a=2\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{2}=\\sqrt{5}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186543878 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6e7a2f97-cecf-54f4-977c-ad480185865c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$37$ and $\\frac{73}{9}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543863} {:answer/id #uuid "82e1672b-a1cf-5e4f-b7ec-400e71ee5eb2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$2\\sqrt{37}$ and $\\frac{2\\sqrt{73}}{3}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543870} {:answer/id #uuid "b3598dd1-c765-5f19-9a18-ce18efdacfdc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$\\sqrt{37}$ and $\\frac{\\sqrt{73}}{3}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543883} {:answer/id #uuid "cb386ed9-a60a-507f-a0a0-3b956d0d46eb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$\\sqrt{37}$ and $\\frac{2\\sqrt{73}}{3}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543889} {:answer/id #uuid "d4ab06df-aa62-554d-b1a3-91a2d87f977e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$2\\sqrt{37}$ and $\\frac{\\sqrt{73}}{3}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543893}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186543870} :answer-field/id #uuid "db39f157-86de-5b5e-aa65-9e3ecfdb909d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543897}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "a8e4213c-472e-5df6-a92b-8b65321f34cc" :question/problem "What are the lengths of the major and minor axes of an ellipse with equation \n$$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{{x}^{2}}{37}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{\\frac{73}{9}}=1?\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that the equation of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn the given equation, we have ${a}^{2}=37$ and ${b}^{2}=\\frac{73}{9}.$\n\n- Since ${a}^{2}=37,$ the length of the horizontal radius must be $a=\\sqrt{37}.$\n\n- Since ${b}^{2}=\\frac{73}{9},$ the length of the vertical radius must be $$\n\\displaystyle b=\\sqrt{\\frac{73}{9}}=\\frac{\\sqrt{73}}{3}.\n$$\n\nSo the semi-major axis has length $a=\\sqrt{37},$ and the semi-minor axis has length $b=\\frac{\\sqrt{73}}{3}.$\n\nTherefore, the major axis has length $2a=2\\cdot \\sqrt{37}=2\\sqrt{37},$ and the minor axis has length $$\n\\displaystyle 2b=2\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{73}}{3}=\\frac{2\\sqrt{73}}{3}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186543886 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0005c1d8-45ea-525d-a5f3-270652f70c50" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$2\\sqrt{53}$ and $\\sqrt{13}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543843} {:answer/id #uuid "346354a8-96ab-5ac3-afa2-7e1332833460" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$2\\sqrt{53}$ and $\\frac{\\sqrt{13}}{2}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543850} {:answer/id #uuid "3b9750c6-a897-50af-bf8f-f7809c90a66d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$53$ and $\\frac{13}{4}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543854} {:answer/id #uuid "40f0a89a-ba5c-5eb5-a4c7-9438df455d0c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$\\sqrt{53}$ and $\\frac{\\sqrt{13}}{2}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543856} {:answer/id #uuid "a21ae6c2-3d2e-5bd5-bda8-5f05bc8b8bc3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$\\sqrt{53}$ and $\\sqrt{13}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543877}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186543843} :answer-field/id #uuid "73b86f1d-d066-5db0-a46a-9f0d01c45177" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543865}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "c3eafdd8-07a8-5eeb-996a-7c5b2fca35e0" :question/problem "What are the lengths of the major and minor axes of an ellipse with equation \n$$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{{x}^{2}}{53}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{\\frac{13}{4}}=1?\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that the equation of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn the given equation, we have ${a}^{2}=53$ and ${b}^{2}=\\frac{13}{4}.$\n\n- Since ${a}^{2}=53,$ the length of the horizontal radius must be $a=\\sqrt{53}.$\n\n- Since ${b}^{2}=\\frac{13}{4},$ the length of the vertical radius must be $$\n\\displaystyle b=\\sqrt{\\frac{13}{4}}=\\frac{\\sqrt{13}}{2}.\n$$\n\nSo the semi-major axis has length $a=\\sqrt{53},$ and the semi-minor axis has length $b=\\frac{\\sqrt{13}}{2}.$\n\nTherefore, the major axis has length $2a=2\\cdot \\sqrt{53}=2\\sqrt{53},$ and the minor axis has length $2b=2\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{13}}{2}=\\sqrt{13}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186543896 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ac8378fb-e823-5792-b0e8-141ffa012178" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\sqrt{47}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543880}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186543880} :answer-field/id #uuid "50ac688e-f2ed-5c1e-b30b-f87af88a4c4e" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543857} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c4f06786-52b2-5301-9c27-c84a9739b589" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543887}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186543887} :answer-field/id #uuid "8c7c7107-1beb-5ce0-af4d-c6cb96a01e5b" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543873}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "d560ab9f-5f69-5c94-a9a1-63ff925308ba" :question/problem "For the ellipse $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{{x}^{2}}{16}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{\\frac{47}{9}}=1,\n$$ find the lengths of its major and minor axes.\n\n$\\,$ The length of the major axis is \n{{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ The length of the minor axis is \n{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "Remember that the equation of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn the given equation, we have ${a}^{2}=16$ and ${b}^{2}=\\frac{47}{9}.$\n\n- Since ${a}^{2}=16,$ the length of the horizontal radius must be $a=\\sqrt{16}=4.$\n\n- Since ${b}^{2}=\\frac{47}{9},$ the length of the vertical radius must be $$\n\\displaystyle b=\\sqrt{\\frac{47}{9}}=\\frac{\\sqrt{47}}{3}.\n$$\n\nThus, the semi-major axis has length $a=4,$ and the semi-minor axis has length $b=\\frac{\\sqrt{47}}{3}.$\n\nTherefore, the major axis has length $2a=2\\cdot 4=8,$ and the minor axis has length $$\n\\displaystyle 2b=2\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{47}}{3}=\\frac{2\\sqrt{47}}{3}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186543898 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6418b296-10d2-5b35-b945-05b31aba2d9e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$2\\sqrt{17}$ and $\\frac{\\sqrt{13}}{2}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543861} {:answer/id #uuid "90328bb5-2efb-5b62-9495-b0a760fef10a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$17$ and $\\frac{13}{4}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543874} {:answer/id #uuid "d3bb7b1f-836e-5b98-9cdd-890c7f95b0ea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$\\sqrt{17}$ and $\\sqrt{13}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543892} {:answer/id #uuid "d502494b-1775-539d-9e06-67be767210e8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$\\sqrt{17}$ and $\\frac{\\sqrt{13}}{2}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543895} {:answer/id #uuid "f7023301-1a20-54bd-92af-1b064d386811" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$2\\sqrt{17}$ and $\\sqrt{13}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543902}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186543902} :answer-field/id #uuid "7347b547-af23-57b6-bf2a-d08e54524091" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543864}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "e487ead8-2208-53bd-a890-504c42fca111" :question/problem "What are the lengths of the major and minor axes of an ellipse with equation \n$$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{{x}^{2}}{\\frac{13}{4}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{17}=1?\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that the equation of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn the given equation, we have ${a}^{2}=\\frac{13}{4}$ and ${b}^{2}=17.$\n\n- Since ${a}^{2}=\\frac{13}{4},$ the length of the horizontal radius must be $$\n\\displaystyle a=\\sqrt{\\frac{13}{4}}=\\frac{\\sqrt{13}}{2}.\n$$\n\n- Since ${b}^{2}=17,$ the length of the vertical radius must be $b=\\sqrt{17}.$\n\nSo the semi-major axis has length $b=\\sqrt{17},$ and the semi-minor axis has length $a=\\frac{\\sqrt{13}}{2}.$\n\nTherefore, the major axis has length $2b=2\\cdot \\sqrt{17}=2\\sqrt{17},$ and the minor axis has length $2a=2\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{13}}{2}=\\sqrt{13}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186543963 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "224855bd-3e30-5d44-a775-59aa99728fa6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "- \\frac{5}{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543968} {:answer/id #uuid "3cab4843-1275-56a8-9e94-45b6860875c7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "- \\frac{1}{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543975} {:answer/id #uuid "4fd35bd8-448c-5d24-835c-e11801044cec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543979} {:answer/id #uuid "51a1f303-9ae9-5719-a96c-ed65308915bd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "- \\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543980} {:answer/id #uuid "da529d28-5c8a-5a5b-a129-84e91c8c5cdb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "- \\frac{1}{30}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544002}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186543975} :answer-field/id #uuid "a051692c-3165-5fdf-a27d-240e91676d01" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543993}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "00a9d1b3-d93b-5510-80b8-e417e1c6daaf" :question/problem "Let $S$ be an oriented piecewise-smooth surface bounded by the closed curve $C$ with positive orientation and parametrization $r(t)=\\langle - t^{2} + t, t^{3} - t^{2}, - 2 t^{2} + 2 t\\rangle$ for $t\\in [0,1).$\nGiven that $F(x,y,z)=\\langle - 2 z, 0, - 3 y\\rangle,$ evaluate the integral\n\n$$\n\\iint_S\\operatorname{curl}F\\cdot\\mathrm{d}S.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let $S$ be an oriented piecewise-smooth surface bounded by a simple, closed, piecewise-smooth boundary $C$ with positive orientation. If a vector field $F$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $S,$ then Stokes' theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr={∬}_{S}\\text{curl}\\,F\\cdot \\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nAlong the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=- t^{2} + t,\\quad y=t^{3} - t^{2},\\quad z=- 2 t^{2} + 2 t\n$$\n\nSo,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t)) & =\\langle - 2 z, 0, - 3 y\\rangle \\\\ & =\\langle - 2 \\left(- 2 t^{2} + 2 t\\right), 0, - 3 \\left(t^{3} - t^{2}\\right)\\rangle \\\\ & =\\langle 4 t^{2} - 4 t, 0, - 3 t^{3} + 3 t^{2}\\rangle\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing $r\\prime(t),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r\\prime(t) & =\\langle \\frac{\\mathrm d}{\\mathrm dt}\\left(- t^{2} + t\\right), \\frac{\\mathrm d}{\\mathrm dt}\\left(t^{3} - t^{2}\\right), \\frac{\\mathrm d}{\\mathrm dt}\\left(- 2 t^{2} + 2 t\\right)\\rangle \\\\ & =\\langle 1 - 2 t, 3 t^{2} - 2 t, 2 - 4 t\\rangle\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t))\\cdot r\\prime(t) & =\\langle 4 t^{2} - 4 t, 0, - 3 t^{3} + 3 t^{2}\\rangle\\cdot \\langle 1 - 2 t, 3 t^{2} - 2 t, 2 - 4 t\\rangle \\\\ & =\\left(4 t^{2} - 4 t\\right)\\cdot \\left(1 - 2 t\\right)+\\left(0\\right)\\cdot \\left(3 t^{2} - 2 t\\right)+\\left(- 3 t^{3} + 3 t^{2}\\right)\\cdot \\left(2 - 4 t\\right) \\\\ & =\\left(- 8 t^{3} + 12 t^{2} - 4 t\\right)+\\left(0\\right)+\\left(12 t^{4} - 18 t^{3} + 6 t^{2}\\right) \\\\ & =12 t^{4} - 26 t^{3} + 18 t^{2} - 4 t \\\\ & =12 t^{4} - 26 t^{3} + 18 t^{2} - 4 t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\iint_S\\operatorname{curl}F\\cdot\\mathrm dS & =\\oint_C F\\cdot\\mathrm dr \\\\ & =\\int_{0}^{1} F(r(t))\\cdot r\\prime(t)\\,\\mathrm dt \\\\ & =\\int_{0}^{1}\\left(12 t^{4} - 26 t^{3} + 18 t^{2} - 4 t\\right)\\,\\mathrm dt \\\\ & =\\left[\\left(- \\frac{13 t^{4}}{2}\\right) + \\left(- 2 t^{2}\\right) + \\left(\\frac{12 t^{5}}{5}\\right) + \\left(6 t^{3}\\right)\\right]_{0}^{1} \\\\ & =\\left(\\left(- \\frac{13}{2}\\right) + \\left(-2\\right) + \\left(\\frac{12}{5}\\right) + \\left(6\\right)\\right)-\\left(\\left(0\\right) + \\left(0\\right) + \\left(0\\right) + \\left(0\\right)\\right) \\\\ & =- \\frac{1}{10}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186543965 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3b3ae743-91b5-55a1-8181-4d006345cf95" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "12 \\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543974} {:answer/id #uuid "79ecbd6c-e61f-5ce9-86c4-e7caec648e2e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543984} {:answer/id #uuid "935747a9-235d-528c-9945-b1717c8e562a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "- 12 \\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543989} {:answer/id #uuid "f0a3acee-6209-5db5-8d34-42ddc39d0af2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "- 6 \\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544007} {:answer/id #uuid "f27e1353-adf9-5977-b05b-7f5737daaa15" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544008}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186543989} :answer-field/id #uuid "c7bac8ec-76be-58f5-9f6a-bfd5a83edd1b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544000}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "07a605d5-0ef0-5420-9db8-ccb3e82bd11b" :question/problem "Let $S$ be an oriented piecewise-smooth surface bounded by the closed curve $C$ with positive orientation and parametrization $r(t)=\\langle 2 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}, 3 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}, 1\\rangle$ for $t\\in [0,2 \\pi).$\nGiven that $F(x,y,z)=\\langle y, - x, 0\\rangle,$ evaluate the integral\n\n$$\n\\iint_S\\operatorname{curl}F\\cdot\\mathrm{d}S.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let $S$ be an oriented piecewise-smooth surface bounded by a simple, closed, piecewise-smooth boundary $C$ with positive orientation. If a vector field $F$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $S,$ then Stokes' theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr={∬}_{S}\\text{curl}\\,F\\cdot \\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nAlong the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=2 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)},\\quad y=3 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)},\\quad z=1\n$$\n\nSo,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t)) & =\\langle y, - x, 0\\rangle \\\\ & =\\langle \\left(3 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}\\right), - \\left(2 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}\\right), 0\\rangle \\\\ & =\\langle 3 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}, - 2 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}, 0\\rangle\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing $r\\prime(t),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r\\prime(t) & =\\langle \\frac{\\mathrm d}{\\mathrm dt}\\left(2 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}\\right), \\frac{\\mathrm d}{\\mathrm dt}\\left(3 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}\\right), \\frac{\\mathrm d}{\\mathrm dt}\\left(1\\right)\\rangle \\\\ & =\\langle - 2 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}, 3 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}, 0\\rangle\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t))\\cdot r\\prime(t) & =\\langle 3 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}, - 2 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}, 0\\rangle\\cdot \\langle - 2 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}, 3 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}, 0\\rangle \\\\ & =\\left(3 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)\\cdot \\left(- 2 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)+\\left(- 2 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)\\cdot \\left(3 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)+\\left(0\\right)\\cdot \\left(0\\right) \\\\ & =\\left(- 6 \\sin^{2}{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)+\\left(- 6 \\cos^{2}{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)+\\left(0\\right) \\\\ & =- 6 \\sin^{2}{\\left(t \\right)} - 6 \\cos^{2}{\\left(t \\right)} \\\\ & =-6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\iint_S\\operatorname{curl}F\\cdot\\mathrm dS & =\\oint_C F\\cdot\\mathrm dr \\\\ & =\\int_{0}^{2 \\pi} F(r(t))\\cdot r\\prime(t)\\,\\mathrm dt \\\\ & =\\int_{0}^{2 \\pi}\\left(-6\\right)\\,\\mathrm dt \\\\ & =\\left[\\left(- 6 t\\right)\\right]_{0}^{2 \\pi} \\\\ & =\\left(\\left(- 12 \\pi\\right)\\right)-\\left(\\left(0\\right)\\right) \\\\ & =- 12 \\pi\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186543967 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2de92522-d011-54d9-8f24-13c4a24e107c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{20}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543970} {:answer/id #uuid "48a048f0-babf-5cea-91be-fe885ae5abd0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543976} {:answer/id #uuid "5c80fd09-835d-527a-858e-3db4b2d132ff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "- \\frac{9}{20}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543981} {:answer/id #uuid "c0f5291a-9fe3-55e4-adc5-