e "\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544027} {:answer/id #uuid "b5c46d77-31d5-59c7-bdd0-8d3efac6744d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544045} {:answer/id #uuid "c9f1390d-33db-59ee-bdda-27ef9c779d1c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544050} {:answer/id #uuid "f9644df6-609d-5092-ba27-53c85a68d464" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\sqrt{6}}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544064}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186544014} :answer-field/id #uuid "c113ca18-6b05-5e8f-850c-b3873ed3519f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544048}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "d41ac9e9-9c5b-5d22-9909-8a38ad72197b" :question/problem "Suppose $|\\,a\\,|=\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{4}$ and the angle between vectors $a$ and $b$ is $\\theta ={150}^{∘}.$ Find the scalar projection of $a$ onto $b.$" :question/worked-solution "Using the formula for the scalar projection, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{comp}}_{b}\\,a & =|\\,a\\,|\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡({150}^{∘}) \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{4}\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right) \\\\  & =-\\frac{3\\sqrt{6}}{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186544065 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2c02b906-ae20-50c8-bed5-3bb797391ce4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{3\\sqrt{3}}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544023}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186544023} :answer-field/id #uuid "cb898007-433e-563f-888f-d36152226b68" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544053}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "fba4bbe3-b689-56be-8219-e32d77251473" :question/problem "Suppose $|\\,a\\,|=\\frac{3}{4}$ and the angle between vectors $a$ and $b$ is $\\theta =\\frac{5\\pi }{6}.$ Find the scalar projection of $a$ onto $b.$\n\n${\\text{comp}}_{b}\\,a=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Using the formula for the scalar projection, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{comp}}_{b}\\,a & =|\\,a\\,|\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}cos\\,\\left(\\frac{5\\pi }{6}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right) \\\\  & =-\\frac{3\\sqrt{3}}{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186544815 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "058f601c-efb8-52f4-ad80-c86475d38425" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2x+3y-2=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544099} {:answer/id #uuid "7f6d7d30-ea91-5f19-ad12-6c285ca3401e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x-y-1=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544366} {:answer/id #uuid "ca4a37f5-406e-5615-bae5-8b7a2a141fbb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x+y-1=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544512} {:answer/id #uuid "d617b5ac-9818-5c3b-ba03-76a6cc05aeca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x+2y-1=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544542} {:answer/id #uuid "e3119854-8196-5825-a5c9-16cfb48b0d16" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x-2y-1=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544576}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186544542} :answer-field/id #uuid "9ced3481-0c5c-5eca-982f-047cab2301fc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544726}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "4e15364c-186a-5328-8307-13438fe58309" :question/math-academy-id "q-130722" :question/problem "Consider the plane curve $\\operatorname{sin}⁡({x}^{2}y)={x}^{2}-{y}^{2}-1.$  Find the equation of the normal line to the curve at the point $(1,0).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we bring all variables to one side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡({x}^{2}y) & ={x}^{2}-{y}^{2}-1 \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡({x}^{2}y)-{x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we set $f(x,y)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡({x}^{2}y)-{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}$, our curve can be viewed as the level curve $f(x,y)=-1.$  \n\nA normal vector to the level curve that passes through the point $(1,0)$ can be found by computing $∇f(1,0).$ \n\nSo, we find the gradient and evaluate it at $(1,0):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & ={f}_{x}(x,y)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y)\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡({x}^{2}y)-{x}^{2}+{y}^{2})i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡({x}^{2}y)-{x}^{2}+{y}^{2})j \\\\  & =(2xy\\operatorname{cos}⁡({x}^{2}y)-2x)i+({x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡({x}^{2}y)+2y)j \\\\ ∇f(1,0) & =(2(1)(0)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(({1}^{2})(0))-2(1))i+((1)^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(({1}^{2})(0))+2(0))j \\\\  & =-2\\,i+j \\\\  & =⟨-2,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $∇f(1,0)=⟨-2,1⟩$ is a normal vector to the curve at $(1,0).$\n\nFinally, the Cartesian equation of the line that is parallel to $v=⟨{v}_{x},{v}_{y}⟩$ and passes through the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0})$ can be written as:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x-{x}_{0}}{{v}_{x}} & =\\frac{y-{y}_{0}}{{v}_{y}} \\\\ \\frac{x-1}{-2} & =\\frac{y-0}{1} \\\\ x-1 & =-2y \\\\ x+2y-1 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186544869 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1262143b-7987-5866-b438-9f2860a6f4b1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544124} {:answer/id #uuid "1e590f80-d8cd-59b5-80b8-713187c9aae9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544153} {:answer/id #uuid "7acbde24-d396-507e-bab6-65ef61f8c0e8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544358} {:answer/id #uuid "b92f50e1-a193-531b-b6de-1ecbee0c7393" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544478} {:answer/id #uuid "ba6eaf80-e94b-55c5-8508-94c9b3e1a5d5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544483}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186544478} :answer-field/id #uuid "1c8cb632-7694-5ff5-badc-7d0d79044a59" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544665}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "b91bd5f9-20da-5be7-a581-8dc4f819e202" :question/math-academy-id "q-90869" :question/problem "If the path $C$ is a part of the curve $y={x}^{3}+1$ traversed from the point $A(0,1)$ to point $B(1,2),$ then $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}x\\,dx+(y-1)\\,dy=\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that our curve is given by the explicit Cartesian equation $y={x}^{3}+1.$ As a result, we obtain the following parametrization of the curve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t)=x(t)\\,i+y(t)\\,j=t\\,i+({t}^{3}+1)\\,j,\\,t\\in [0,1]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe derivative of $r(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j=i+3{t}^{2}\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now evaluate the line integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}x\\,dx+(y-1)\\,dy & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}x\\,\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t+(y-1)\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(x\\,\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+(y-1)\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}t\\cdot 1+({t}^{3}+1-1)\\cdot 3{t}^{2}\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}t+3{t}^{5}\\,dt \\\\  & ={[\\frac{{t}^{2}}{2}+\\frac{{t}^{6}}{2}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =[\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{1}{2}]-[0] \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186545075 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "56aff325-7b07-5275-8c12-1b2e8759ff4e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186545085} {:answer/id #uuid "6add7eb0-9ea7-5148-a402-b2bdc51fbb6d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186545091} {:answer/id #uuid "792b7082-6807-5196-9601-1d26374888cd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "30" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186545095} {:answer/id #uuid "adaa1cc7-706e-598c-b2ec-5be249f3d877" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186545099} {:answer/id #uuid "ccc345c3-aeee-5369-a84c-30e8f9cb2d22" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "180" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186545102}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186545091} :answer-field/id #uuid "e2236ae3-1e28-597d-a5b3-9b0419cdd9c7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186545107}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "07e55509-b25a-54e5-8c3a-f3a8ce4459c4" :question/problem "Find the volume bounded by the region $R,$ given by\n\n$$\nR=\\big\\{(x,y,z)\\, :\\, 0\\le 2 x\\le 5,\\quad 0\\le - 3 x + y\\le 2,\\quad 0\\le 3 z\\le 3\\big\\}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the volume of $R$ can be found as \n\n$$\nV={∭}_{R}\\text{d}V.\n$$\n\nLet's define a transformation $T$ as follows:\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v,w) \\\\ y(u,v,w) \\\\ z(u,v,w)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uvw$-space to our region $R$ in the $xyz$-space.\n\nTo compute the required volume, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\text{ }dV={∭}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v,w),y(u,v,w),z(u,v,w))\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nThe inequalities that define the region $R,$\n\n$$\n0\\le 2 x\\le 5,\\quad 0\\le - 3 x + y\\le 2,\\quad 0\\le 3 z\\le 3,\n$$\n\nsuggest that we use the change of variables\n\n$$\nu=2 x,\\quad v=- 3 x + y,\\quad w=3 z.\n$$\n\nThis change of variables gives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\nT^{-1}:(x,y,z)\\longmapsto(u(x,y,z),v(x,y,z),w(x,y,z)).\n$$\n\nTo compute the required volume, we proceed in four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $R$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$\n\nOur domain in the $uvw$-space is\n\n$$\n\\Delta=\\big\\{(u,v,w):0\\le u\\le 5,\\quad 0\\le v\\le 2,\\quad 0\\le w\\le 3\\big\\}.\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\partial(u,v,w)}{\\partial(x,y,z)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{\\partial u}{\\partial x} & \\frac{\\partial u}{\\partial y} & \\frac{\\partial u}{\\partial z} \\\\ \\frac{\\partial v}{\\partial x} & \\frac{\\partial v}{\\partial y} & \\frac{\\partial v}{\\partial z} \\\\ \\frac{\\partial w}{\\partial x} & \\frac{\\partial w}{\\partial y} & \\frac{\\partial w}{\\partial z}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\left(2\\right)(-1)^{1+1}\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{vmatrix}+\\left(0\\right)(-1)^{1+2}\\begin{vmatrix}-3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{vmatrix}+\\left(0\\right)(-1)^{1+3}\\begin{vmatrix}-3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\partial(x,y,z)}{\\partial(u,v,w)}=\\left(\\frac{\\partial(u,v,w)}{\\partial(x,y,z)}\\right)^{-1}=\\frac{1}{6}.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\nThe map to $R$ is\n\n$$\nT(u,v,w)=\\left(\\frac{u}{2},\\frac{3 u}{2} + v,\\frac{w}{3}\\right).\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Perform the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\iiint_R\\mathrm{d}V & =\\iiint_\\Delta 1\\cdot |\\frac{1}{6}|\\,\\mathrm{d}u\\mathrm{d}v\\mathrm{d}w \\\\  & =\\int_{0}^{5}\\int_{0}^{2}\\int_{0}^{3}\\frac{1}{6}\\,\\mathrm{d}w\\,\\mathrm{d}v\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}\\cdot \\int_{0}^{5}1\\,\\mathrm{d}u\\cdot \\int_{0}^{2}1\\,\\mathrm{d}v\\cdot \\int_{0}^{3}1\\,\\mathrm{d}w \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}\\cdot [u]_{0}^{5}\\cdot [v]_{0}^{2}\\cdot [w]_{0}^{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}\\cdot \\left(\\left(\\left(5\\right)\\right)-\\left(\\left(0\\right)\\right)\\right)\\cdot \\left(\\left(\\left(2\\right)\\right)-\\left(\\left(0\\right)\\right)\\right)\\cdot \\left(\\left(\\left(3\\right)\\right)-\\left(\\left(0\\right)\\right)\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}\\cdot \\left(\\left(5\\right)-\\left(0\\right)\\right)\\cdot \\left(\\left(2\\right)-\\left(0\\right)\\right)\\cdot \\left(\\left(3\\right)-\\left(0\\right)\\right) \\\\  & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186545077 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0d3fce2d-b0fb-5de7-956f-6f1597c9fe19" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186545078} {:answer/id #uuid "463e4f8d-3236-5dea-988d-12549f60bab4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186545083} {:answer/id #uuid "6f72fe8e-20ee-5bf9-8652-252c8f94140c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "40" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186545092} {:answer/id #uuid "74a2023b-70f4-5988-b24e-a88339e0fa2f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186545093} {:answer/id #uuid "e19c619e-1028-574d-92ee-f6105319a63d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "160" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186545106}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186545078} :answer-field/id #uuid "fb445c12-48c4-530f-b2ed-dc019c34045f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186545109}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "0c37e2fc-f4eb-5061-a3a1-4d88ea3a6767" :question/problem "Find the volume bounded by the region $R,$ given by\n\n$$\nR=\\big\\{(x,y,z)\\, :\\, 0\\le 2 x\\le 5,\\quad 0\\le 2 x + y\\le 2,\\quad 0\\le 2 z\\le 4\\big\\}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the volume of $R$ can be found as \n\n$$\nV={∭}_{R}\\text{d}V.\n$$\n\nLet's define a transformation $T$ as follows:\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v,w) \\\\ y(u,v,w) \\\\ z(u,v,w)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uvw$-space to our region $R$ in the $xyz$-space.\n\nTo compute the required volume, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\text{ }dV={∭}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v,w),y(u,v,w),z(u,v,w))\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nThe inequalities that define the region $R,$\n\n$$\n0\\le 2 x\\le 5,\\quad 0\\le 2 x + y\\le 2,\\quad 0\\le 2 z\\le 4,\n$$\n\nsuggest that we use the change of variables\n\n$$\nu=2 x,\\quad v=2 x + y,\\quad w=2 z.\n$$\n\nThis change of variables gives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\nT^{-1}:(x,y,z)\\longmapsto(u(x,y,z),v(x,y,z),w(x,y,z)).\n$$\n\nTo compute the required volume, we proceed in four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $R$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$\n\nOur domain in the $uvw$-space is\n\n$$\n\\Delta=\\big\\{(u,v,w):0\\le u\\le 5,\\quad 0\\le v\\le 2,\\quad 0\\le w\\le 4\\big\\}.\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\partial(u,v,w)}{\\partial(x,y,z)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{\\partial u}{\\partial x} & \\frac{\\partial u}{\\partial y} & \\frac{\\partial u}{\\partial z} \\\\ \\frac{\\partial v}{\\partial x} & \\frac{\\partial v}{\\partial y} & \\frac{\\partial v}{\\partial z} \\\\ \\frac{\\partial w}{\\partial x} & \\frac{\\partial w}{\\partial y} & \\frac{\\partial w}{\\partial z}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\left(2\\right)(-1)^{1+1}\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{vmatrix}+\\left(0\\right)(-1)^{1+2}\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{vmatrix}+\\left(0\\right)(-1)^{1+3}\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\partial(x,y,z)}{\\partial(u,v,w)}=\\left(\\frac{\\partial(u,v,w)}{\\partial(x,y,z)}\\right)^{-1}=\\frac{1}{4}.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\nThe map to $R$ is\n\n$$\nT(u,v,w)=\\left(\\frac{u}{2},- u + v,\\frac{w}{2}\\right).\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Perform the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\iiint_R\\mathrm{d}V & =\\iiint_\\Delta 1\\cdot |\\frac{1}{4}|\\,\\mathrm{d}u\\mathrm{d}v\\mathrm{d}w \\\\  & =\\int_{0}^{5}\\int_{0}^{2}\\int_{0}^{4}\\frac{1}{4}\\,\\mathrm{d}w\\,\\mathrm{d}v\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot \\int_{0}^{5}1\\,\\mathrm{d}u\\cdot \\int_{0}^{2}1\\,\\mathrm{d}v\\cdot \\int_{0}^{4}1\\,\\mathrm{d}w \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot [u]_{0}^{5}\\cdot [v]_{0}^{2}\\cdot [w]_{0}^{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot \\left(\\left(\\left(5\\right)\\right)-\\left(\\left(0\\right)\\right)\\right)\\cdot \\left(\\left(\\left(2\\right)\\right)-\\left(\\left(0\\right)\\right)\\right)\\cdot \\left(\\left(\\left(4\\right)\\right)-\\left(\\left(0\\right)\\right)\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot \\left(\\left(5\\right)-\\left(0\\right)\\right)\\cdot \\left(\\left(2\\right)-\\left(0\\right)\\right)\\cdot \\left(\\left(4\\right)-\\left(0\\right)\\right) \\\\  & =10\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186545098 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5c2f499d-3b71-54c8-878c-df1526bd355e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{20}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186545087} {:answer/id #uuid "671f0fae-58af-5409-b296-def7ac5666b5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "40" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186545089} {:answer/id #uuid "6aa7b22e-94ea-562c-97e1-b8f1143c580d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "240" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186545090} {:answer/id #uuid "a8dd97d3-4024-52d7-9d5c-689d01e28958" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186545097} {:answer/id #uuid "d840e2e5-8bba-581d-994e-de4f7cacc8e5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186545105}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186545087} :answer-field/id #uuid "5a0da8d7-797d-527f-b2d1-fa1fc9fbc734" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186545086}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "acb9920a-c4c3-5163-83aa-da5aa673fe37" :question/problem "Find the volume bounded by the region $R,$ given by\n\n$$\nR=\\big\\{(x,y,z)\\, :\\, 0\\le 3 x\\le 5,\\quad 0\\le x + y\\le 4,\\quad 0\\le 2 z\\le 2\\big\\}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the volume of $R$ can be found as \n\n$$\nV={∭}_{R}\\text{d}V.\n$$\n\nLet's define a transformation $T$ as follows:\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v,w) \\\\ y(u,v,w) \\\\ z(u,v,w)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uvw$-space to our region $R$ in the $xyz$-space.\n\nTo compute the required volume, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\text{ }dV={∭}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v,w),y(u,v,w),z(u,v,w))\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nThe inequalities that define the region $R,$\n\n$$\n0\\le 3 x\\le 5,\\quad 0\\le x + y\\le 4,\\quad 0\\le 2 z\\le 2,\n$$\n\nsuggest that we use the change of variables\n\n$$\nu=3 x,\\quad v=x + y,\\quad w=2 z.\n$$\n\nThis change of variables gives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\nT^{-1}:(x,y,z)\\longmapsto(u(x,y,z),v(x,y,z),w(x,y,z)).\n$$\n\nTo compute the required volume, we proceed in four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $R$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$\n\nOur domain in the $uvw$-space is\n\n$$\n\\Delta=\\big\\{(u,v,w):0\\le u\\le 5,\\quad 0\\le v\\le 4,\\quad 0\\le w\\le 2\\big\\}.\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\partial(u,v,w)}{\\partial(x,y,z)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{\\partial u}{\\partial x} & \\frac{\\partial u}{\\partial y} & \\frac{\\partial u}{\\partial z} \\\\ \\frac{\\partial v}{\\partial x} & \\frac{\\partial v}{\\partial y} & \\frac{\\partial v}{\\partial z} \\\\ \\frac{\\partial w}{\\partial x} & \\frac{\\partial w}{\\partial y} & \\frac{\\partial w}{\\partial z}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\left(3\\right)(-1)^{1+1}\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{vmatrix}+\\left(0\\right)(-1)^{1+2}\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{vmatrix}+\\left(0\\right)(-1)^{1+3}\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\partial(x,y,z)}{\\partial(u,v,w)}=\\left(\\frac{\\partial(u,v,w)}{\\partial(x,y,z)}\\right)^{-1}=\\frac{1}{6}.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\nThe map to $R$ is\n\n$$\nT(u,v,w)=\\left(\\frac{u}{3},- \\frac{u}{3} + v,\\frac{w}{2}\\right).\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Perform the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\iiint_R\\mathrm{d}V & =\\iiint_\\Delta 1\\cdot |\\frac{1}{6}|\\,\\mathrm{d}u\\mathrm{d}v\\mathrm{d}w \\\\  & =\\int_{0}^{5}\\int_{0}^{4}\\int_{0}^{2}\\frac{1}{6}\\,\\mathrm{d}w\\,\\mathrm{d}v\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}\\cdot \\int_{0}^{5}1\\,\\mathrm{d}u\\cdot \\int_{0}^{4}1\\,\\mathrm{d}v\\cdot \\int_{0}^{2}1\\,\\mathrm{d}w \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}\\cdot [u]_{0}^{5}\\cdot [v]_{0}^{4}\\cdot [w]_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}\\cdot \\left(\\left(\\left(5\\right)\\right)-\\left(\\left(0\\right)\\right)\\right)\\cdot \\left(\\left(\\left(4\\right)\\right)-\\left(\\left(0\\right)\\right)\\right)\\cdot \\left(\\left(\\left(2\\right)\\right)-\\left(\\left(0\\right)\\right)\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}\\cdot \\left(\\left(5\\right)-\\left(0\\right)\\right)\\cdot \\left(\\left(4\\right)-\\left(0\\right)\\right)\\cdot \\left(\\left(2\\right)-\\left(0\\right)\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{20}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186545101 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0b358110-f098-5b00-b994-090f1909a25c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186545076} {:answer/id #uuid "51890235-6f06-5902-9890-b9a7fdb916d2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "60" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186545084} {:answer/id #uuid "5f6cd16e-dd2d-5a8f-b2ca-b029c56b79b6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "15" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186545088} {:answer/id #uuid "7511abe9-750b-5ffd-b849-345cffb8b183" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186545094} {:answer/id #uuid "8f8ee32e-d4e4-5d12-86b8-17197e316325" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "240" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186545096}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186545088} :answer-field/id #uuid "15249b49-ff93-5f2c-93db-24c193e0cd42" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186545079}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "c7d71c23-13c3-5551-bd04-90d1004e74ff" :question/problem "Find the volume bounded by the region $R,$ given by\n\n$$\nR=\\big\\{(x,y,z)\\, :\\, 0\\le 2 x\\le 6,\\quad 0\\le - 2 x + y\\le 5,\\quad 0\\le 2 z\\le 2\\big\\}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the volume of $R$ can be found as \n\n$$\nV={∭}_{R}\\text{d}V.\n$$\n\nLet's define a transformation $T$ as follows:\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v,w) \\\\ y(u,v,w) \\\\ z(u,v,w)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uvw$-space to our region $R$ in the $xyz$-space.\n\nTo compute the required volume, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\text{ }dV={∭}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v,w),y(u,v,w),z(u,v,w))\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nThe inequalities that define the region $R,$\n\n$$\n0\\le 2 x\\le 6,\\quad 0\\le - 2 x + y\\le 5,\\quad 0\\le 2 z\\le 2,\n$$\n\nsuggest that we use the change of variables\n\n$$\nu=2 x,\\quad v=- 2 x + y,\\quad w=2 z.\n$$\n\nThis change of variables gives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\nT^{-1}:(x,y,z)\\longmapsto(u(x,y,z),v(x,y,z),w(x,y,z)).\n$$\n\nTo compute the required volume, we proceed in four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $R$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$\n\nOur domain in the $uvw$-space is\n\n$$\n\\Delta=\\big\\{(u,v,w):0\\le u\\le 6,\\quad 0\\le v\\le 5,\\quad 0\\le w\\le 2\\big\\}.\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\partial(u,v,w)}{\\partial(x,y,z)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{\\partial u}{\\partial x} & \\frac{\\partial u}{\\partial y} & \\frac{\\partial u}{\\partial z} \\\\ \\frac{\\partial v}{\\partial x} & \\frac{\\partial v}{\\partial y} & \\frac{\\partial v}{\\partial z} \\\\ \\frac{\\partial w}{\\partial x} & \\frac{\\partial w}{\\partial y} & \\frac{\\partial w}{\\partial z}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\left(2\\right)(-1)^{1+1}\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{vmatrix}+\\left(0\\right)(-1)^{1+2}\\begin{vmatrix}-2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{vmatrix}+\\left(0\\right)(-1)^{1+3}\\begin{vmatrix}-2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\partial(x,y,z)}{\\partial(u,v,w)}=\\left(\\frac{\\partial(u,v,w)}{\\partial(x,y,z)}\\right)^{-1}=\\frac{1}{4}.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\nThe map to $R$ is\n\n$$\nT(u,v,w)=\\left(\\frac{u}{2},u + v,\\frac{w}{2}\\right).\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Perform the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\iiint_R\\mathrm{d}V & =\\iiint_\\Delta 1\\cdot |\\frac{1}{4}|\\,\\mathrm{d}u\\mathrm{d}v\\mathrm{d}w \\\\  & =\\int_{0}^{6}\\int_{0}^{5}\\int_{0}^{2}\\frac{1}{4}\\,\\mathrm{d}w\\,\\mathrm{d}v\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot \\int_{0}^{6}1\\,\\mathrm{d}u\\cdot \\int_{0}^{5}1\\,\\mathrm{d}v\\cdot \\int_{0}^{2}1\\,\\mathrm{d}w \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot [u]_{0}^{6}\\cdot [v]_{0}^{5}\\cdot [w]_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot \\left(\\left(\\left(6\\right)\\right)-\\left(\\left(0\\right)\\right)\\right)\\cdot \\left(\\left(\\left(5\\right)\\right)-\\left(\\left(0\\right)\\right)\\right)\\cdot \\left(\\left(\\left(2\\right)\\right)-\\left(\\left(0\\right)\\right)\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot \\left(\\left(6\\right)-\\left(0\\right)\\right)\\cdot \\left(\\left(5\\right)-\\left(0\\right)\\right)\\cdot \\left(\\left(2\\right)-\\left(0\\right)\\right) \\\\  & =15\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186545103 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1e0387f6-7891-536d-89bd-6333fa70803b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186545080} {:answer/id #uuid "1e5d776d-779f-5abd-a919-03eb9abf0ea5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "36" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186545081} {:answer/id #uuid "b3291ed3-3ec0-53ab-98e4-742596316faa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186545100} {:answer/id #uuid "d405320f-ac4a-5ded-a0f7-1c88f1044077" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "144" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186545104} {:answer/id #uuid "f987ea8f-2d47-5bcb-9aec-3b002eb91f54" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186545108}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186545100} :answer-field/id #uuid "2fbae90c-1929-5df1-973c-3b25dfec911e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186545082}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "cf55ae1c-a342-5187-901c-0388815d197c" :question/problem "Find the volume bounded by the region $R,$ given by\n\n$$\nR=\\big\\{(x,y,z)\\, :\\, 0\\le 2 x\\le 2,\\quad 0\\le x + y\\le 3,\\quad 0\\le 2 z\\le 6\\big\\}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the volume of $R$ can be found as \n\n$$\nV={∭}_{R}\\text{d}V.\n$$\n\nLet's define a transformation $T$ as follows:\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v,w) \\\\ y(u,v,w) \\\\ z(u,v,w)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uvw$-space to our region $R$ in the $xyz$-space.\n\nTo compute the required volume, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\text{ }dV={∭}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v,w),y(u,v,w),z(u,v,w))\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nThe inequalities that define the region $R,$\n\n$$\n0\\le 2 x\\le 2,\\quad 0\\le x + y\\le 3,\\quad 0\\le 2 z\\le 6,\n$$\n\nsuggest that we use the change of variables\n\n$$\nu=2 x,\\quad v=x + y,\\quad w=2 z.\n$$\n\nThis change of variables gives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\nT^{-1}:(x,y,z)\\longmapsto(u(x,y,z),v(x,y,z),w(x,y,z)).\n$$\n\nTo compute the required volume, we proceed in four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $R$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$\n\nOur domain in the $uvw$-space is\n\n$$\n\\Delta=\\big\\{(u,v,w):0\\le u\\le 2,\\quad 0\\le v\\le 3,\\quad 0\\le w\\le 6\\big\\}.\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\partial(u,v,w)}{\\partial(x,y,z)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{\\partial u}{\\partial x} & \\frac{\\partial u}{\\partial y} & \\frac{\\partial u}{\\partial z} \\\\ \\frac{\\partial v}{\\partial x} & \\frac{\\partial v}{\\partial y} & \\frac{\\partial v}{\\partial z} \\\\ \\frac{\\partial w}{\\partial x} & \\frac{\\partial w}{\\partial y} & \\frac{\\partial w}{\\partial z}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\left(2\\right)(-1)^{1+1}\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{vmatrix}+\\left(0\\right)(-1)^{1+2}\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{vmatrix}+\\left(0\\right)(-1)^{1+3}\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\partial(x,y,z)}{\\partial(u,v,w)}=\\left(\\frac{\\partial(u,v,w)}{\\partial(x,y,z)}\\right)^{-1}=\\frac{1}{4}.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\nThe map to $R$ is\n\n$$\nT(u,v,w)=\\left(\\frac{u}{2},- \\frac{u}{2} + v,\\frac{w}{2}\\right).\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Perform the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\iiint_R\\mathrm{d}V & =\\iiint_\\Delta 1\\cdot |\\frac{1}{4}|\\,\\mathrm{d}u\\mathrm{d}v\\mathrm{d}w \\\\  & =\\int_{0}^{2}\\int_{0}^{3}\\int_{0}^{6}\\frac{1}{4}\\,\\mathrm{d}w\\,\\mathrm{d}v\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot \\int_{0}^{2}1\\,\\mathrm{d}u\\cdot \\int_{0}^{3}1\\,\\mathrm{d}v\\cdot \\int_{0}^{6}1\\,\\mathrm{d}w \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot [u]_{0}^{2}\\cdot [v]_{0}^{3}\\cdot [w]_{0}^{6} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot \\left(\\left(\\left(2\\right)\\right)-\\left(\\left(0\\right)\\right)\\right)\\cdot \\left(\\left(\\left(3\\right)\\right)-\\left(\\left(0\\right)\\right)\\right)\\cdot \\left(\\left(\\left(6\\right)\\right)-\\left(\\left(0\\right)\\right)\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot \\left(\\left(2\\right)-\\left(0\\right)\\right)\\cdot \\left(\\left(3\\right)-\\left(0\\right)\\right)\\cdot \\left(\\left(6\\right)-\\left(0\\right)\\right) \\\\  & =9\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186548280 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0ab6fa70-a6cd-5cb7-9d73-3596ed01243a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "- 81 \\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548274} {:answer/id #uuid "19c9f542-d2e2-5968-bded-427e0d8db6a3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "144 \\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548281} {:answer/id #uuid "21f78820-0932-5d64-87e6-27386a744f06" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "54 \\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548285} {:answer/id #uuid "249d1b9e-afcc-5de7-be41-64c8e545e5c1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548286} {:answer/id #uuid "728cf46f-850c-5bf2-8eee-8591a6dfdd10" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "81 \\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548303}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186548303} :answer-field/id #uuid "ebe1fb6b-3741-520d-8e93-709e401053a3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548332}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "14caf42e-c7a2-5980-adda-a477fd9e1d63" :question/problem "Let $C$ be the intersection of the cylinder $x^2+y^2=9$ and the plane $z=3$. The curve $C$ is oriented counter-clockwise when viewed from above. Given that $\\text{curl}\\,F=\\langle - x z,\\,- y z,\\,x + z^{2}\\rangle$, use Stokes' theorem to evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle \\oint_C F\\cdot\\mathrm{d}r.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let $S$ be an oriented piecewise-smooth surface bounded by a simple, closed, piecewise-smooth boundary $C$ with positive orientation. If a vector field $F$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $S,$ then Stokes' theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr={∬}_{S}\\text{curl}\\,F\\cdot \\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nThere are many surfaces whose boundary is the curve $C.$ One such surface is our graph $S$ with the equation\n\n$$z=g(x,y)=3.$$\n\nWe first compute the partial derivatives of the graph $z=g(x,y):$\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\partial g}{\\partial x}&=\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial x}\\left(3\\right)=0\\\\\n\\frac{\\partial g}{\\partial y}&=\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial y}\\left(3\\right)=0.\\end{aligned}$$\n\nNext, we substitute the equation $z=g(x,y)$ into the expression for $\\text{curl}\\,F:$\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}\\text{curl}\\,F|_{z=g(x,y)}&=\\langle - x z,\\,- y z,\\,x + z^{2}\\rangle|_{z=3}\\\\\n&=\\langle - 3 x,\\,- 3 y,\\,x + 9\\rangle\\\\\n&=\\langle - 3 x,\\,- 3 y,\\,x + 9\\rangle.\\end{aligned}$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}\\iint_S\\text{curl}\\,F\\cdot\\mathrm{d}S&=\\iint_D(-P g_x-Q g_y+R)\\,\\mathrm{d}A\\\\\n&=\\iint_D\\left(-\\left(- 3 x\\right)\\left(0\\right)-\\left(- 3 y\\right)\\left(0\\right)+\\left(x + 9\\right)\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}A\\\\\n&=\\iint_D\\left((0)+(0)+(x + 9)\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}A\\\\\n&=\\iint_D\\left(x + 9\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}A.\\end{aligned}$$\n\nwhere $D$ is the region enclosed by the projection of $C$ onto the $xy$-plane.\nNotice that the projection of $C$ onto the $xy$-plane is the circle $x^2+y^2=9.$ Therefore, using the change of variables to polar coordinates\n\n$$x=r\\cos\\theta,\\quad y=r\\sin\\theta,\\quad \\mathrm{d}A=r\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta,$$\n\nwe obtain\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}\\iint_D\\left(x + 9\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}A\n&=\\int_0^{3}\\int_0^{2\\pi}\\left(r \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)} + 9\\right)r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\\\\n&=\\int_0^{3}\\int_0^{2\\pi}\\left(r^{2} \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)} + 9 r\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\\\\n&=\\int_0^{3}\\left[r^{2} \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)} + 9 r \\theta\\right]_0^{2\\pi}\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\\\\n&=\\int_0^{3}\\left[\\left(18 \\pi r\\right)-\\left(0\\right)\\right]\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\\\\n&=\\int_0^{3}\\left(18 \\pi r\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\\\\n&=\\left[9 \\pi r^{2}\\right]_0^{3}\\\\\n&=\\left[\\left(81 \\pi\\right)\\right]-\\left[\\left(0\\right)\\right]\\\\\n&=81 \\pi.\\end{aligned}$$\n"}]}
